4|0@prolife^1440029469#cu8x1ga:cantenucci04$:I have thought about this a lot, I've been active in the pro-life movement for 10 years, since I was 19. I've researched and written about this topic extensively. It's not an emotional argument for me, but a logical, scientific, and of course moral/religious one. Regarding the heart and being born alive, the doctor mentioned in the article is an expert on stem cell research and developmental biology, and she stated clearly that in order for PP to obtain the hearts in the conditions they described, the fetus would have to be late-term, and even possibly born alive in order to preserve the heart tissue, which can't last long once a baby dies. If you don't agree that biological life is important to the issue then that's where we have a fundamental disagreement because as a Christian my faith tells me that life begins at conception and that from that moment on it is inviolable and sacred, meaning it was given by God and must not be destroyed by man. Biologically, when comparing someone in a coma without brain waves to a fetus without a fully developed brain, there is no difference, that's why the comparison is valid. You're comparing life experience and other things from the adult to the fetus, but that's irrelevant because at that moment when he or she loses consciousness and goes into the coma, he or she is in the exact same mental state that a fetus with no brain waves is in. Therefore we have to treat them as the same situation, which means the same questions will arise and the same ethical treatment must be applied. Also, to answer your question, we're morally obligated to keep alive any human at any stage of life who has a chance to continue living. A human born with no brain would die immediately and thus not fall into that category. But we also wouldn't kill any baby that would eventually die on its own, which is why intentions matter. It's the same thing with someone in a coma. Once their brain activity stops, we aren't obligated to keep them alive forever because they would die, and taking them off life support would simply facilitate this natural death, which is the opposite of abortion, where the killing is anything but natural, and where the fetus would continue living if allowed to. 
