aith to tell you that. I've never met anyone who disagreed with this even though most pro-life people will insist that they exist somewhere. &gt;from that moment on it is inviolable and sacred Sacred: "Devoted or dedicated to a deity or to some religious purpose". Obviously I am not going to agree. Inviolable: "prohibiting violation; secure from destruction, violence, infringement, or desecration:". Merely begging the question here, this is claim that must be supported not an a priori truth that can be accepted without question. &gt;meaning it was given by God and must not be destroyed by man Yep, and here we have religion corrupting morality by claiming things are true without providing a rationale behind them and in fact denying that such rationale is required. &gt;Biologically, when comparing someone in a coma without brain waves to a fetus without a fully developed brain, there is no difference, that's why the comparison is valid. I agree it's valid, however it's not an identical situation. The fetus, according to it's own perspective, doesn't even exist yet (or more accurately the fetus does not have a perspective). The person in a coma already has a life (distinct from merely being alive) to return to, friends and family and ambitions. That is significant. The fetus doesn't even exist yet from it's own perspective (as it has no perspective) so preventing the development of it's self awareness is not practically different than doing so by abstaining from sex. Every time you don't have sex you are preventing the development of self awareness of a human that could have resulted from getting pregnant... there is no difference that is significant to the ethics of the issue. &gt;You're comparing life experience and other things from the adult to the fetus, but that's irrelevant because at that moment when he or she loses consciousness and goes into the coma, he or she is in the exact same mental state that a fetus with no brain waves is in. Therefore we have to treat them as the same situation No we don't, and frankly I don't understand how you and others fail to understand the difference between a person with a life and a fetus who does not have a life (note that I am using the word "life" differently than the biological sense, having a life means having a personality, having ambitions, having loved ones, having obligations, etc). &gt;we're morally obligated to keep alive any human at any stage of life who has a chance to continue living Begging the question. This is a claim by you, this is not a fact, and as such you must support it. For what it's worth the majority of professional ethicists strongly disagree with you (in fact I'd bet that almost all of them do). &gt;A human born with no brain would die immediately and thus not fall into that category. Different parts of the brain are responsible for different things. You can be born with only a the small primal part responsible for autonomous life-preserving functions while having no brain capable of thought or consciousness. I'm pretty sure this actually happens but I'd have to look it up. --- I think most of you are entirely missing the point of the coma analogy... it is meant to show that BIOLOGICAL LIFE is not the only thing significant to having a meaningful existence as a human. I cringe every time I hear "life begins at conception"... yeah, it does, SO WHAT? Life is not the pertinent issue, life is not what gives humans value or meaning. A meaningful human existence must also consist of self awareness, which is why we kill people if they are in a coma and we don't expect them to recover, because while they are still biologically alive they have no meaningful human life... I'm honestly not sure why that's such a hard concept to understand here, is it because your religious beliefs cause you all to think so differently than I do?
