0|0@prolife^1440168539#cuap2wa:ameliachristie$:&gt;Don't you see that this is merely your own personal opinion? It's a scientific fact that "you" as a person don't exist when that which ultimately leads to you is merely a unicellular zygote. That's not a matter of opinion. &gt;Biological we know that a new human being exists. You have to understand that words like "human being" are somewhat arbitrarily defined... the entire concept of species is arbitrarily defined and there are several competing definitions that are each equally valid depending on what we deem important, arbitrarily. When a "human" exists or does not exist depends entirely on what you mean by "human"... if you simply mean a life form with human DNA then you're correct... but calling a unicellular zygote a "human" is a huge stretch. There is no hard and fast line where a millisecond prior it is not a human and a millisecond later it is, it's a subjective, definitional, arbitrary thing we are talking about. The problem is we try to use words with concrete meaning but EVERY single second in the development of the fetus it is a different "thing", we don't have enough words to use to accurately talk about it so we say "it's either a human or it's not" and this is imprecise. No one talks about this issue with precise enough language so we all end up arguing semantics over what "human" means when there is no objective definition, only individual subjective opinions, and that is why the argument never ends. I don't think it's appropriate to call a unicellular zygote a "human", but if you asked me to pinpoit the exact millisecond in the development of the fetus where it becomes a human I wouldn't be able to answer that, clearly it's a human at college graduation and clearly (in my opinion) it is not when it is a single cell (it's a cell with human DNA, but that's not enough to be called a human in the same sense as the human standing on stage receiving their diploma). &gt;Does a tree exist? From an outside 