1|0@prolife^1440251702#cubs48z:treasurece$:Firstly the term arbitrary means, based on random choice or personal whim, rather than any reason or system. This does not apply to the term human being which has been defined by biology as belonging to the species homo sapiens. We can use the scientific method (which can hardly be considered arbitrary) to determine whether a thing is a human. Through observation of the natural phenomenon occurring and by assigning criteria that must be consistently applicable to things of that nature. It is really a very simple concept. 1.) An embryo is human by virtue of his or her dna. 2) An embryo is alive by virtue of demonstrating the seven characteristics of life. 3.) An embryo is a being by virtue of the fact that he/she acts like an organism. All the other things you are discussing pertain to philosophy and not science. You are focusing on assigning value based on your own personal criteria rather than science. That is arbitrary. My question was, does a tree exist? Not whether or not the tree is aware of it's own existence. A newborn is not self aware yet we know that they certainly exist. Those are two different things entirely. Now it seems you are placing value on self awareness rather than consciousness. Think about it, according to me at the time that I am anesthetized I also don't exist. Yet it does not follow logically that I actually do not (in reality) exist because at the time I have no perceptive, self awareness or consciousness. This is starting to sound like a sad attempt at defending a position that has been ingrained in you instead of a well thought out opinion. I am sorry (correct me if I am wrong) but I must say I am starting to doubt your intentions for being on this sub.
